      "content": [
        {
          "type": "toolResponse",
          "id": "call_00_JrZgCTumO3yAkeA4R0uD7497",
          "toolResult": {
            "status": "success",
            "value": {
              "resultType": "complete",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "# Loaded: lore-self-career-index/references/contracting-offers.md\n\n# contracting offers, speaking palette\n\nBuilt 2026-08-25 for Kai to speak from, not to publish. **This is prep material.** Outer wording is Gem's and goes through the website tickets. Rate is $150 an hour at 70% utilisation, confirmed 2026-08-24, recorded in `lore-self-compensation`.\n\nSame five slots every time so the position is reachable under pressure: their problem, what it costs, what it does, the line that lands, where it came from.\n\n**The headline set changed on 2026-08-28** (`coilyco-bridge/inbox#477`): umbra, beaver, housecast, acompose. sirens-echo keeps its cells below as a real offer and stops being a headline. housecast's first four cells are drafted from its shipped surface, and its fifth is open because the origin line is Kai's to say.\n\n## umbra, the wall itself\n\n* **Their problem** - the agent has a shell, and you are trusting it not to do something stupid with it\n* **What it costs** - one bad tool call reaches something nobody scoped for it, and your orchestrator cannot tell you whether the agent was blocked or the tool just broke\n* **What it does** - argv validated before execve, a scope token per verb, egress through a per-invocation proxy, every call appended to an audit log, and a published exit-code taxonomy separating policy refusal from tool failure\n* **The line that lands** - the whole wall is one file a reviewer reads in a sitting, and it ships no denylist because the policy is yours\n* **Where it came from** - I would not let anything run unattended until I could answer what it was allowed to touch and what it actually touched\n\n## mcp-beaver, write access without the blast radius\n\n* **Their problem** - you want to give an agent write access to your issue tracker or your infrastructure, and you cannot bound what it reaches\n* **What it costs** - every MCP server becomes bespoke code someone has to review, and nobody reviews it properly the second time\n* **What it does** - one generic image, many guardfiles. Declare operations down to the leaf, and an undeclared operation has no tool and no endpoint at all instead of a guarded one\n* **The line that lands** - audit one small file, hand an agent a write-capable server, and know the blast radius before it runs\n* **Where it came from** - I needed the same thing for six different services and refused to write it six times\n\n## agent-compose, the right specialist instantly, on any harness\n\n* **Their problem** - every session starts by re-explaining your own conventions to a fresh generalist\n* **What it costs** - you get a generalist when you needed a specialist, every time, and the tax is highest exactly when you are already tired\n* **What it does** - one command materializes a named specialist with its own voice, its own set of things it hands off instead of attempts, and its own tool inventory. The same pack runs unchanged on Claude Code, Codex, Goose, and OpenCode\n* **The line that lands** - the personality is not decoration. It changes what the agent notices first and what it refuses to do, and the roster is measured instead of asserted\n* **Where it came from** - I wanted to stop explaining myself every morning, and I wanted the thing I was talking to to have a point of view\n\n## housecast, one roster, and the board comes with it\n\n* **Their problem** - each agent role lives in its own prompt file, and by the seventh role nobody can say which one owns a given capability\n* **What it costs** - the boundary between two roles gets stated twice, in two files, in different words, and nothing in the system disagrees with either version\n* **What it does** - one YAML roster declares the roles, personalities, boundaries, and the invariant. The engine validates it, resolves each role's meld and boundary allocation, emits an immutable bundle, then runs the behavior evals against exactly that bundle\n* **The line that lands** - one file is the source, and the board is a consequence of it. The graded artifact and the shipped artifact are the same thing\n* **Where it came from** - open. Kai's to say\n\n## sirens-echo, a community agent people actually talk to\n\n* **Their problem** - your community agent is boring, so nobody asks it anything, and a bot nobody talks to is worse than no bot\n* **What it costs** - the usual fix is sanding the voice down until it is safe, which is what made it boring. Neutrality reads as utility instead of capability, especially to a technical audience\n* **What it does** - one harness, two configurations differing in voice and nothing else. Identical bounds underneath either way\n* **The line that lands** - safety comes from evaluating identity and relationships, not from removing the personality. Voice reduction is not a control\n* **Where it came from** - questions it cannot answer become tracked issues, so the community tells you what its documentation is missing without anyone filing anything\n\n## What takes an offer off the table\n\nRecorded 2026-08-29. Section 1532 of the FY2026 NDAA is law and in force. It prohibits DoD and Intelligence Community contractors, **and their subcontractors**, from using \"Covered AI\" while performing the contract. Covered AI reaches any model developed by a company domiciled in or subject to the ownership, control, or influence of China, Russia, North Korea, or Iran, and any entity on Commerce's Consolidated Screening List. Waivers are case-by-case from the Secretary of Defense only.\n\n* **What it catches** - sirens-echo Deep runs on hosted DeepSeek, so the demo behind the sirens-echo offer is Covered AI. The US-hosted open-weights fallback does not cure it, because the statute keys on who developed the model rather than on who serves it\n* **Who it reaches** - anyone in a federal defense or intelligence contract chain, prime or sub. The test is the chain instead of whether the client calls itself a defense company\n* **What it leaves alone** - umbra, mcp-beaver, housecast, and agent-compose ship no model, so all four are unaffected and stay sayable in the same room\n* **The move** - ask whether a prospect performs on federal contracts before describing any stack. If yes, lead with the four headlines and treat sirens-echo as unavailable instead of explaining why\n* **Not a general ban** - no US law reaches a private individual or an ordinary commercial client as of mid-2026. Device bans cover federal and some state government devices, and H.R.1121 has not passed\n\n**Watch instead of fact.** Lawmakers were probing US company use of Chinese models in July 2026, and reporting says a commercial restriction is being prepared. If that lands, this stops being a contracting question and becomes a routing one, and the standing call to leave Deep on its current route gets revisited.\n\n**Settled, do not re-run.** Anthropic's AUP training clause, Commercial Terms D.4, and Consumer Terms section 3 were all read directly on 2026-08-29 and none is engaged, because nothing in the estate trains or tunes weights. The binding mechanism here is procurement law, not a terms-of-service question. Egress scope was tracked at `coilyco-flight-deck/agentic-os#1429`, a Forgejo record kept as provenance rather than a link.\n\n## Using it\n\n**The fifth cell converts.** Origin says the thing was built against a real problem rather than as a portfolio piece, and it sounds least like sales. **Drop the third cell first** under time pressure. Mechanism is what Kai most wants to say and what a buyer needs least. Given two cells, use the first and the fourth.\n\n**All of them are one offer, demonstrated once per project**: constrain what an agent can do, and prove what it did. Separate offers make a buyer choose, which is friction. One offer with several proofs answers the only follow-up that matters.\n\n## Open\n\n**sirens-echo is the weakest cell for contracting, and it is no longer a headline.** The four headlines sell to a team running agents against their own systems. This one sells to whoever owns a community, a different buyer with a usually smaller budget. It may be the demo instead of the offer. Decide deliberately instead of carrying it because it was on the homepage.\n\n**The unanswered-questions-become-issues mechanic may be its whole offer.** A community generating its own documentation backlog is a sentence a buyer understands instantly and has nothing to do with agent safety.\n\nRelated: `lore-self-credential-legibility` for why practitioner surfaces miss buyers, `lore-self-career-direction` for the ADU gate on full independence.\n\n\n---\nFile loaded into context."
                }
              ],
              "isError": false
            }
          }
        }
