              "resultType": "complete",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "# Loaded: lore-project-index/references/portfolio.md\n\n# project portfolio\n\nKai's portfolio centers on governed agent systems. **No single flagship project is established.** The strongest story is the way several independently useful layers compose into a working agent platform, then show up in real infrastructure and community applications.\n\n**Forgejo is the canonical current-state inventory.** An old note, skill, website page, GitHub repo, or local checkout **does not make a project active.** Confirm the active repo set on Forgejo before describing the portfolio externally.\n\n## Lanes\n\n**Agent systems core** - four headlines carry this lane, committed by Kai on 2026-08-28 (`coilyco-bridge/inbox#477`): **umbra, beaver, housecast, acompose**. **umbra** is the occlusion engine and the renamed successor to cli-guard, shipping the `umbra` CLI (called `specgen` until v0.192.0). **mcp-beaver** renders umbra guardfiles into guarded MCP servers, and is **infrastructure rather than a product**: thirteen named gateways now convert APIs to MCP tools and apply policy there, so the generation half is commoditized and the product line is closed (2026-09-02). What survives is guarded execution inside the runtime on infrastructure the operator owns, which the other three lean on. **housecast** is the roster engine and eval runner: one YAML roster composes every role's bundle and the board that grades it. **agent-compose** sits downstream and renders that bundle into harness surfaces without executable authority. `lore-stack-agent-systems` owns the exact boundaries, AOS and AOSguard included.\n\n**Ward is archived, not a current lane.** Kai confirmed on 2026-08-28: archived rather than deleted, configs being deprovisioned. The `ward:` workflow lane vocabulary survives in AGENTS.md frontmatter and is read by composition. **Do not present Ward as active work.**\n\n**Models, hardware, and serving** - agentic-os-hardware owns machine profiles, benchmarks, model scoreboards, and the tower reliability plan. Agent Proxy is the reliability and routing layer in front of local serving, with LiteLLM and the tower runtimes below the harness layer. **Activating Agent Proxy is the career campaign's target**, because it captures the trajectories an eval measures against and Kai rates it severely underutilized. That is activation rather than construction, and it does **not** make it a fifth headline. Its adoption blocker is cheap and unmoved: verified 2026-09-03, the repository is public with no LICENSE file and no homepage, so nobody can adopt the artifact the target rests on. Row `teable:coilyco-flight-deck/agent-proxy#1563`, sitting at P3, which is the wrong price for it.\n\n**Platform and deploy** - infrastructure covers host convergence, k3s, fleet policy, release machinery, and reachability. Deploy is the application-plane monorepo owning service manifests, rollout contracts, and the shared observability plane. **Atlas is being retired across the estate**, at `teable:coilyco-bridge/agentic-os-kai#2230`. It is absent from canonical Forgejo and still live on GitHub, and two surfaces still assume it is alive. The disposition is decided and only the propagation is outstanding, so this is a sweep rather than a question. It was never the authority on whether a repo is active.\n\nThe topology separates app and ops: kai-server is the application plane, ser8 carries observability and supporting operations.\n\n**Applied systems** - Sirens Echo as an applied Community harness with bounded Discord invocation and narrow authority, not a headline. **Sirens Echo is also the single subject behind every agent-behaviour claim the portfolio makes** - not the estate, not the fleet, not the cluster. That n-of-one ceiling bounds all of them, every new claim inherits the bound rather than testing it, and a second operated subject is the only thing that raises it. Gaming covers Eco application and operations, Eco and Factorio mods, Steam operations, galaxy-gen, and factory-game-v3. **These are real software and community surfaces, not supporting examples for a fictional company.**\n\n**Personal namespace** - the `coilysiren` namespace holds the profile, website, inbox, and lore. **The website can lag current project state.**\n\n## Archived-name guardrail\n\n**Gauntlet, LUCA, repo-recall, session-lattice, o2r, and Coily are not current portfolio layers.** Old references to a dark-factory trio, a local session substrate, an autonomous relay, or a Coily command wall describe archived states.\n\n**Do not revive those names when explaining current work. Do not infer activity from an archived GitHub repo or a stale generated registry.**\n\n## Current narrative\n\nLead with the four headlines: policy in umbra, a guarded tool surface in mcp-beaver, the roster and its eval board in housecast, and the rendered bundle in acompose. Then inference, deploy, and the community harness. Describe Gaming as a parallel active lane. **Do not claim a flagship, business, customer base, or single product unless newer evidence establishes one.**\n\n\n---\nFile loaded into context."
                }
              ],
              "isError": false
