        {
          "type": "thinking",
          "thinking": "I now have the key facts. Let me synthesize.\n\nKey confirmed facts:\n- Rule rate is $150/hour at 70% utilisation (confirmed 2026-08-24). This is the single confirmed rule rate, \"a rate rather than a floor, and no walk-away, premium tier or negotiation ladder is confirmed.\"\n- Floor $200K otherwise, $170K nonprofit/gov, $250K top anchor. But \"a rate and a salary floor are different units and do not compare\" — I must not derive an annual equivalent or compare.\n- The four headlines: umbra, mcp-beaver, housecast, acompose. acompose = \"agent-compose\" in the contracting cells.\n- umbra = the occlusion engine / wall itself (argv validation, scope token per verb, egress proxy, audit log, exit-code taxonomy).\n- acompose = renders the housecast bundle into harness surfaces without executable authority; \"the right specialist instantly, on any harness.\"\n- There is no flagship, no business, no customer base, no single product. Do not claim customers.\n- The fifth cell converts (origin). Drop third cell (mechanism) under time pressure.\n- \"All of them are one offer, demonstrated once per project.\"\n\nThe question: \"What might someone pay for a custom one-time cut plus long-term support of acompose or umbra? Give me price bands for each, and say whether anyone would actually buy one.\"\n\nImportant anti-invention discipline: I must not invent comp preferences. The only confirmed rate is $150/hr at 70% utilisation. No walk-away, no premium tier, no negotiation ladder confirmed. So I should NOT invent bands beyond what's confirmed. But the user is asking me to produce price bands. This is a productized-service / consulting pricing question, which is different from Kai's salary comp.\n\nThe comp skill is about Kai's salary/FTE comp. There's also the household skill (lore-self-household-index) which has the break-even rate $137/hr — but that's not loaded. Actually, the question here is about pricing acompose/umbra as a productized service. This is a business question.\n\nLet me think about what's confirmed vs what I'd be inventing:\n- The rule rate $150/hr is confirmed.\n- 70% utilisation confirmed.\n- No walk-away, no premium tier, no negotiation ladder confirmed — so I should not invent a premium tier.\n\nBut \"custom one-time cut plus long-term support\" is a specific offering shape (one-time build + retainer). That's not confirmed anywhere. The discipline: \"Do not invent comp preferences.\" But this is pricing of a productized service, not Kai's comp. Still, the same anti-invention discipline should apply: I shouldn't fabricate a number that Kai hasn't set.\n\nAlso, importantly — the portfolio says: \"Do not claim a flagship, business, customer base, or single product unless newer evidence establishes one.\" And acompose and umbra are open-source headlines in the portfolio, not currently products with customers.\n\nThere's also the \"business-disposition.md\" in lore-self-career-index — Kai's founding impulse, \"product-type-before-financing rule.\" And \"saas-wedges.md\" — twelve SaaS wedges. And there's the \"tooling-scout-autonomy\" / build things. And \"lore-self-household-index\" for break-even.\n\nActually, the question about \"would anyone actually buy one\" — I should check the contracting-offers \"which cells convert\" (fifth cell converts), and there's the \"credential-legibility.md\" — \"the at-a-glance credential gap, the technical-buyer audience mismatch.\" That's relevant to \"would anyone buy.\"\n\nLet me also consider loading lore-self-household-index for the break-even rate, and business-disposition.md and saas-wedges.md for the pricing/wedge context.\n\nActually, the question is fairly direct and I have strong material. But to answer \"what might someone pay\" accurately and not invent, I should ground in:\n1. The confirmed rate: $150/hr, 70% utilisation. This is the only confirmed pricing anchor.\n2. The household break-even: $137/hr (in lore-self-household-index) — relevant for \"what Kai needs.\"\n3. The no-invention rule: I must not fabricate bands. I can say \"the only confirmed anchor is $150/hr; any band I give beyond that is my inference, flagged as such.\"\n\nLet me load lore-self-household-index for the break-even rate, and business-disposition.md + saas-wedges.md for the \"would anyone buy\" answer. Actually the break-even rate $137/hr is in the trigger list for lore-self-household-index. Let me load it.\n\nAlso the guardrail: I'm prod-director with unsycophantic critique + decisive + outward. The outward personality: \"WebSearch the comparison before any claim that something is good, slow, expensive, novel, or popular, and report the result count.\" And \"would anyone buy\" is a market claim that should be checked against the outside. The role run says: \"WebSearch the claim before ranking it, and report how many independent results came back.\"\n\nBut wait — this is a question about Kai's own projects and pricing, and I have lore (internal authoritative source). The outward check would be for market comparables (e.g., what do people pay for agent-security tooling, custom dev + retainer). That's a genuine outside check. Given I have web-search tool available, and the outward personality says to check the outside before a market claim (\"would anyone actually buy one\"), I should do a web search on market comparables.\n\nActually, let me think about the guardrail discipline. The guardrail is \"unsycophantic critique\": strongest case against first, reversal observation named, verdict from separate decision model.\n\nThe question has three parts:\n1. Price bands for custom one-time cut + long-term support of acompose.\n2. Price bands for umbra.\n3. Would anyone actually buy one?\n\nLet me gather remaining context efficiently. I'll load:\n- lore-self-household-index (break-even $137/hr)\n- lore-self-career-index/references/business-disposition.md (founding impulse, product-type-before-financing)\n- lore-self-career-index/references/credential-legibility.md (buyer mismatch — would anyone buy)\n- lore-project-index/references/saas-wedges.md (the wedge ranking — is this a wedge, what's the pricing envelope)\n\nActually let me be efficient. The most relevant for \"would anyone buy\" and \"price\" are:\n- business-disposition.md (product-type-before-financing rule — directly relevant to \"would anyone buy\" a custom cut)\n- saas-wedges.md (wedge ranking, envelope corrections)\n- household break-even ($137/hr)\n- credential-legibility.md (buyer mismatch)\n\nLet me load these.",
