    {
      "id": "msg_9092eb9e-ace5-4441-b24a-15b1c39b0005",
      "role": "user",
      "created": 1790144076,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "toolResponse",
          "id": "call_01_GFQMzrd6brJKIaP6oRi76820",
          "toolResult": {
            "status": "success",
            "value": {
              "resultType": "complete",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "# Loaded: lore-self-career-index/references/business-disposition.md\n\n# business disposition\n\nKai's interest in building a business predates the current agent-tooling cycle. The relevant prior is CollectQT / Quirell, a queer and trans social-network prototype she built in 2014 and 2015.\n\n## Why the prior matters\n\nKai reads that attempt as constrained by solo development throughput, not by a lack of conviction in the problem. Current coding agents and a decade of skill growth change that constraint materially. The founding impulse is a long-running disposition. New tools make another attempt more feasible, but the tools did not create the disposition.\n\n## Product type before financing form\n\nKai starts with the thing worth creating. Bootstrapped product, productized service, contracting, or venture financing follow from the product's capital and scale needs. Some products force a financing form. Most do not need that decision before the problem and user are concrete.\n\n## Founding as a transition state\n\nA second and distinct motive sits alongside the founding impulse. Founding a company produces a credential that hiring teams read directly, and senior hiring conversations quote founder experience as evidence that a team is strong. The line carries weight independent of whether the company succeeds.\n\nKai holds this as a live option: found something while expecting it to fail, and take the credential and operating experience as the return.\n\n**Keep the two motives separate.** A business pursued because the problem is worth solving is evaluated by the rule below. A business pursued as a transition state is evaluated on whether the credential and experience are worth the time and money at risk. **Name which of the two is in play instead of dressing a transition-state attempt in conviction language.**\n\n## Evaluation rule\n\nGoverns a business pursued as a business. When an idea looks like a candidate, test:\n\n1. whether Kai cares about the problem beyond the novelty of building it\n2. whether current throughput changes the earlier bottleneck\n3. whether the product itself forces a particular capital form\n4. what evidence from a new build would update the throughput estimate\n\n**Do not promote a possible path into an established company, customer base, or offering without evidence.**\n\nRelated: `lore-self-credential-legibility` for the container the transition-state motive is answering.\n\n\n---\nFile loaded into context."
                }
              ],
              "isError": false
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "userVisible": true,
        "agentVisible": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "msg_777c74dc-dfb2-42df-8e55-a24449c4f289",
      "role": "user",
      "created": 1790144076,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "toolResponse",
          "id": "call_02_ogkUBctahBXclSsd6e221878",
          "toolResult": {
            "status": "success",
            "value": {
              "resultType": "complete",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "# Loaded: lore-self-career-index/references/credential-legibility.md\n\n# credential legibility\n\nThe gap Kai hits at staff level in AI-adjacent companies is not academic. It is engineering credentials that read at a glance to an evaluator who cannot assess the engineering directly. Her substantive work already covers bounded execution environments, evaluation harnesses, and verification as infrastructure. What it lacks is a container that carries the work into a room before a technical evaluation happens.\n\n**This is a positioning problem, not a skills problem.** Recommending more building, more certification, or a broader stack does not address it. Neither does more volume in the existing practitioner formats. `coilyco-bridge/inbox#299` reached the same conclusion, and is kept as provenance rather than a link: the gap is a recruiter-visible proof package, not missing skill or missing open-source history.\n\nFounding a company is one available container. `lore-self-business-disposition` records the transition-state motive that follows.\n\n## The audience side\n\nKai's current answer is the code freeze and live streams and talks. Those surfaces speak to **workers**. Technical **buyers** are a different audience: a buyer wants a problem they already have, named in their own terms, with the cost of leaving it unfixed attached. That is a written artifact carrying a consequence, not a demonstration of capability. Kai's own read is that the current surfaces do not go far enough toward buyers. The formats work. The audience they aim at is the mismatch.\n\n## Reading interview outcomes\n\nSeparate the two readings. **A credential gap named by the hiring side is evidence. A template-grade recruiter screen reflects nothing back and is not evidence about Kai's standing either way.**\n\nValidated 2026-08-15. Kai read the Ode intro call as zero chance on engineering-credential grounds. Ode passed her forward to a technical. The screen carried no signal in either direction, exactly as the rule predicts, and the rule applies against Kai's own pessimistic read and not only against a rejection. Do not re-derive a probability from a low-information screen, and do not repeat a stale one back once the stage has moved.\n\nRelated: `lore-self-career-positioning`, `lore-self-career-direction`.\n\n\n---\nFile loaded into context."
                }
              ],
              "isError": false
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "userVisible": true,
        "agentVisible": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "msg_78b6f17b-0320-4ead-810c-207f0c244218",
