        {
          "type": "thinking",
          "thinking": "I have strong material now. Let me also check the household break-even rate figure and the homestead-findings for the break-even $137/hr, which is directly relevant to \"what Kai needs.\" Actually, the homestead-findings.md has the break-even rate. Let me load it. Also the financial baseline for run-rate. But I should be careful: the break-even rate is a household-plan finding, not a pricing anchor. Still it's relevant context for \"what Kai needs to clear.\"\n\nLet me load homestead-findings.md to get the break-even rate precisely, and financial-baseline for context. Actually, I have enough. The key break-even figure is $137/hr per the trigger list. But I should confirm the exact number before quoting it, since the guardrail discipline (lore-rule-source-authority) says don't quote without checking. Let me load homestead-findings.md.\n\nActually, let me weigh efficiency vs completeness. The question is a pricing question. My answer needs:\n\n1. Price bands for acompose (agent-compose) — custom one-time cut + long-term support.\n2. Price bands for umbra — same.\n3. Would anyone actually buy one?\n\nGrounded anchors I have:\n- Rule rate: $150/hr at 70% utilisation. This is THE confirmed pricing anchor.\n- No walk-away, no premium tier, no negotiation ladder confirmed → I must not invent these.\n- Floor $200K / $170K nonprofit-gov / $250K top anchor, but \"rate and salary floor are different units and do not compare\" → don't derive annual equivalents.\n- Household break-even $137/hr (need to confirm).\n- Portfolio: no flagship, no business, no customers, no single product. Do not claim customers.\n- Business disposition: \"Do not promote a possible path into an established company, customer base, or offering without evidence.\" Product type before financing form.\n- contracting-offers: one offer, five cells, fifth cell converts. \"All of them are one offer.\"\n- saas-wedges: envelope — steady-state bar is a ceiling test, figure derives from independent rate in lore-self-compensation, not restated. On-call is a moat (support is actually attractive to Kai, reliability-shaped). \"Only the ADU is closed to outside money.\"\n- credential-legibility: technical buyers are a different audience; current surfaces (code, streams, talks) speak to workers, not buyers. This is relevant to \"would anyone buy\" — the mismatch is the audience, and there's no buyer-facing artifact naming the cost of leaving the problem unfixed.\n\nNow the key question of \"what might someone pay\" — I must be careful. The user is asking me to produce price bands. This is a productized service pricing question. There's no confirmed price band for \"custom one-time cut + long-term support\" anywhere in lore. The only confirmed number is $150/hr.\n\nThe anti-invention discipline is about comp preferences. Pricing a service is adjacent but distinct. Still, I should be honest: the only confirmed anchor is $150/hr. I can construct bands AS INFERENCE clearly labeled, not as Kai's position.\n\nActually, let me think about the guardrail and my role more carefully. This is a decision-support question (\"what might someone pay... would anyone buy\"). As prod-director, my job is to name the decision and its gate, give the strongest case against first, name the reversal, and take a verdict from a separate decision model.\n\nThe outward personality also says: WebSearch the comparison before any market claim (\"would anyone buy\"), and report result count. I have web-search. Should I actually run web searches? The question is partly internal (what's Kai's rate) and partly external (what does the market pay for custom agent-security/dev tooling + support, and is there demand).\n\nGiven the outward mandate and the guardrail, I should do at least a couple of web searches to ground the \"would anyone buy\" and market-price claim. Let me do that. But I also need to be mindful of budget and not over-search.\n\nActually — let me reconsider. The instruction says \"WebSearch the comparison before any claim that something is good, slow, expensive, novel, or popular, and report the result count.\" And \"would anyone buy\" is a market/demand claim. I should check the outside.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: this is a fairly contained question and the lore already has strong internal signals (credential-legibility says buyers are a mismatch; saas-wedges says mcp-beaver is closed as a product; the portfolio says no customers). The honest \"would anyone buy\" answer is mostly \"there is no evidence anyone has bought, and the current surfaces don't reach buyers.\"\n\nLet me do a focused web search to ground market comparables for: (1) what custom dev shops charge for a one-time integration/build plus a support retainer, and (2) whether there's a real market for agent-security / agent-permission tooling. This satisfies the outward mandate.\n\nLet me do 2-3 web searches. Actually I have a `web-search` extension listed in the skill catalog, but do I have a web-search TOOL in my actual tool list? Let me check my available tools.\n\nMy tools: analyze, apps__*, delegate, edit, extensionmanager__*, kai-server MCP (create_choice/noul/score decisions, mcp_beaver_info), load, load_skill, read_image, shell, todo, tree, write.\n\nThere's NO web-search tool in my actual function list. The \"web-search\" is listed as a skill in the system prompt (\"web-search - Search the web...\"), but it's not in my function list. Wait, actually it's listed under the skills section, not as a function. Let me check — the functions available are the ones with JSON schemas at the end. Web search is not among them.\n\nSo I cannot actually WebSearch. The outward personality's \"WebSearch\" run attribute can't be executed here because there's no web tool. I should be honest about that — \"checked none\" is an explicit option the outward tell allows: \"says plainly that it checked none.\"\n\nActually wait — is there a web-search skill I could load that gives instructions? The system prompt lists \"web-search\" as a skill. But even loading it wouldn't give me a tool unless the skill wires up a tool. And I don't see a web search tool. Let me not over-engineer. I'll note plainly that I checked no outside source (no web tool available), and ground my answer in the lore as the authoritative internal source.\n\nHmm, but actually let me reconsider — maybe I should try. There's no web_search function in my toolset. I'll be honest.\n\nActually, the guardrail + decisive + outward personality wants me to be explicit. Let me structure the answer properly.\n\nLet me confirm the break-even rate before quoting. Load homestead-findings.md.\n\nActually, I want to be careful about quoting household financial figures. The household skill trigger says \"the per-ledger figure that must not be quoted\" (in homestead-salary-lock.md) and \"$2533k, 4376k\" etc. The break-even rate $137/hr is a finding. Is it safe to quote? It's Kai's private financial data. But the question is about what someone might pay, and the break-even is relevant to \"what Kai needs.\" However, this is a private fact, and I should be cautious about surface level. The lore-rule-disclosure-gradient says the lore overlay is never public. But the user here is presumably Kai (or her agent seat). The question is phrased as a decision question about her own projects.\n\nI think it's fine to reference the break-even rate as an internal anchor for \"what the floor needs to clear,\" since the user is asking about pricing Kai's own offerings. But I should confirm the exact number. Let me load homestead-findings.md.\n\nLet me load it to confirm $137/hr and get context on what it means (probably the rate at which the household plan breaks even / metamour can retire at 45, etc.).",
