      "content": [
        {
          "type": "thinking",
          "thinking": "I now have the break-even rate confirmed: $137/hr at 70% (against $200k salary lock), recomputed 2026-09-13.\n\nNote: this is \"Category 1 on lore-rule-disclosure-gradient\" — the Wall says no public artifacts. But this is an internal conversation. The household financial data (break-even, utilisation fragility) is private. I should be careful: the user is presumably Kai or her seat, asking about pricing her own work. I can reference the break-even rate internally as a floor anchor, but I should not over-share the metamour's finances (the $137/hr is Kai's own break-even, that's fine; the metamour exit age / who fails is not relevant here and I won't touch it).\n\nNow let me construct the answer. I have everything I need. No web tool available, so I'll be honest that I checked no outside source.\n\nLet me think about the actual substance of the answer now.\n\n## What are acompose and umbra (for pricing context)?\n\n- **acompose (agent-compose)**: renders the housecast roster bundle into harness surfaces (Claude Code, Codex, Goose, OpenCode) without executable authority. One command materializes a named specialist with voice, boundaries, tool inventory. It's the \"rendered bundle\" — the top of the stack, the surface layer. It depends on housecast upstream.\n- **umbra**: the occlusion engine / the wall itself — argv validated before execve, scope token per verb, per-invocation egress proxy, audit log, published exit-code taxonomy. It's the renamed successor to cli-guard, shipping the `umbra` CLI. It's the policy/enforcement layer — the deepest, most security-relevant layer.\n\nKey distinctions for pricing:\n- umbra is security-critical, sits at execve/egress — a buyer is paying to bound blast radius. This is the highest-value, highest-scrutiny piece. It's the one that maps to \"what does it cost to leave it unfixed\" = one bad tool call reaching something unscoped. Security spend.\n- acompose is a DX/productivity layer — \"stop re-explaining your conventions to a fresh generalist.\" Lower ceiling than umbra per the wedge analysis; more of a productivity tool. But it's the most \"sellable to a team\" as it's the surface people touch.\n\n## The confirmed pricing anchor\n\n- $150/hr at 70% utilisation. That's THE rule rate. Not a floor, no walk-away, no premium tier, no negotiation ladder confirmed.\n- Break-even against the $200k salary lock: $137/hr at 70%. So $150/hr clears break-even by ~$13/hr.\n- \"Rate and salary floor are different units and do not compare.\" Don't derive annual equivalents.\n\n## The \"custom one-time cut + long-term support\" shape\n\nThis is NOT a confirmed offering. The lore says:\n- \"Do not claim... single product\" / no customer base.\n- Business disposition: \"product type before financing form\" — bootstrapped product, productized service, contracting, or venture follow from the product's capital needs.\n- The contracting-offers is \"prep material\" — five cells, one offer, fifth cell converts.\n- saas-wedges: steady-state bar is a ceiling test; on-call is a moat (support is attractive, not a burden); only the ADU is closed to outside money.\n\nSo \"custom one-time cut + long-term support\" is a specific engagement shape: a one-time scoped build/cut (project) + a recurring support retainer. This is essentially \"productized service / contracting.\" It's not confirmed anywhere, so any price band I give is inference, not Kai's number.\n\n## The guardrail: strongest case against first\n\nStrongest case against giving these bands at all:\n1. There's no confirmed price band. The only number is $150/hr. Every band I produce is my invention, and the comp discipline explicitly bans inventing a \"premium tier\" or \"negotiation ladder.\" A \"one-time cut + long-term support\" package with a published band is precisely a negotiation ladder Kai hasn't set.\n2. There's no buyer and no buyer-facing artifact. credential-legibility: current surfaces (code, streams, talks) speak to workers, not buyers. No artifact names the buyer's problem + cost-of-inaction. So \"would anyone buy\" → no evidence anyone would, today.\n3. mcp-beaver was closed as a product because the market crowded (13 gateways). That's a warning: the guard/enforcement space commoditized fast. umbra could face the same.\n4. The wedge analysis: \"three of the four are one bet\" — need the agent market to keep deepening. acompose especially depends on housecast upstream (it renders housecast's bundle) — selling acompose custom cuts means selling the whole stack's maintenance, a support liability.\n\nReversal observation that would change the call: evidence of a buyer — someone who actually has the problem (an agent with a shell they don't trust) and named the cost of leaving it unfixed, or an inbound warm introduction spent on this. Also a buyer-facing written artifact.\n\nVerdict from separate decision model: I'm supposed to take the verdict from a separate decision model rather than generate it. There's the Jev MCP (tailnet_coilyco_jev / kai-server_30089_mcp). Actually I have `kai-server_30089_mcp__create_score_decision` etc. The guardrail says \"the verdict taken from a separate decision model rather than generated here.\" Should I call Jev? \n\nThe kai-server_30089_mcp provides create_choice_decision, create_noul_decision, create_score_decision, and mcp_beaver_info. This is the Jev TypeSafe System One typed decision through Agent Proxy.\n\nHmm. The guardrail says \"a number this seat produced on its own\" is a defect. But the Jev decision tools are for typed decisions (choice/noul/score), \"interactive decisions only.\" And these are \"policy-approved tools\" — \"Use read-only tools to inspect state before mutation tools.\" The decision tools go upstream.\n\nShould I actually invoke Jev for the \"would anyone buy\" verdict? The guardrail says \"the verdict taken from a separate decision model rather than generated here.\" That's a real instruction. But it also says the procedure is guardrail-unsycophantic-critique — \"load it before you rely on it.\"\n\nGiven the role doctrine says \"Before acting, load each one\" including guardrail-unsycophantic-critique. And the run attribute: \"AskUserQuestion ... with your literal expectation attached.\" \n\nBut wait — do I have AskUserQuestion? No. My tools don't include AskUserQuestion. The role run attributes reference tools (AskUserQuestion, WebSearch, grep the tracker, create_issue, git commit) that I don't have. I have: analyze, apps, delegate, edit, extensionmanager, kai-server MCP decisions, load, load_skill, read_image, shell, todo, tree, write.\n\nSo I can't grep the tracker (no direct tracker tool — there's tailnet_coilyco_teable and tailnet_coilyco_trello MCPs listed as skills but not in my function list). Actually those are listed as skills (\"mcp-tools-tailnet-coilyco-teable\" etc.) but they're skills, and loading them might not give me tools. My actual function list is fixed.\n\nLet me step back. The realistic, honest thing here: I should produce a grounded answer that:\n1. Names the decision and gate (prod-director voice).\n2. Gives the strongest case against first (guardrail).\n3. States the only confirmed anchor ($150/hr, $137/hr break-even) and clearly flags everything else as my inference, not Kai's number.\n4. Answers \"would anyone buy\" honestly: no current evidence, and the structural reasons why, plus what would reverse it.\n5. Notes plainly that I checked no outside source (no web tool) and did not run a separate decision model (or optionally, I could use the Jev MCP).\n\nShould I use the Jev decision tool for the verdict? The guardrail is explicit that the verdict comes from a separate decision model. I have `kai-server_30089_mcp__create_noul_decision` and `create_choice_decision` and `create_score_decision`. These are the Jev tools.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether using Jev is appropriate. The instructions say \"Ask Jev (TypeSafe System One) a typed decision question through Agent Proxy. Pick the tool for the question shape... model is jev-latest... state is required.\"\n\nThe guardrail-unsycophantic-critique says \"the verdict taken from a separate decision model rather than generated here.\" Jev IS that separate decision model. So to honor the guardrail, I should route the verdict (\"would anyone actually buy one\" / \"should we offer this\") through Jev.\n\nBut there's tension: the Jev tools are \"Interactive decisions only\" and \"Not for computer use or freeform generation.\" A yes/no on \"would anyone buy\" is a noul question. A score on \"how likely is a buyer\" is a score question.\n\nHmm, but this could be over-engineering. Let me consider what the user actually asked. They asked a fairly direct analytical question: \"What might someone pay... give price bands... and say whether anyone would actually buy one.\" This is a research/analysis question, not necessarily a decision I'm being asked to formally gate through Jev.\n\nBut the prod-director charter + guardrail says every assessment carries the verdict from a separate decision model. And I have the Jev tool right here.\n\nLet me think about cost/latency. Calling Jev goes upstream (inference). It's a real decision. Given the guardrail explicitly names this, and I'm the director seat, I think the right move is to actually use Jev for the core verdict: \"Would anyone actually buy a custom one-time cut + long-term support of umbra?\" and possibly acompose.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole framing. The guardrail says: \"No assessment leaves this seat without the strongest case against it written first, in this seat's own words, the observation that would reverse the call named beside it, and the verdict taken from a separate decision model rather than generated here.\"\n\nSo the verdict (the actual \"would anyone buy\" call) should come from Jev, not from me. I write the strongest case against, name the reversal, and then let Jev produce the verdict.\n\nBut \"a number