gration, exit-code taxonomy mapping. Realistically a few days to a couple weeks. Say 40–160 hours. At $150/hr = $6k–$24k one-time.\n- acompose: a custom cut = a new role/roster bundle rendered to a client's harness — smaller, but it depends on housecast. If housecast is the platform, a custom cut is authoring a roster + validating. Say 16–80 hours = $2.4k–$12k.\n\nLong-term support retainer:\n- Monthly retainer at some hours/month. Say 5–20 hrs/month = $750–$3k/month.\n- Annualized that's $9k–$36k/yr. But I shouldn't derive annual equivalents per the \"rate and salary floor are different units\" rule? That rule is specifically about salary floor vs rate comparison. A retainer annualization is fine to state as a range for a recurring service, but I'll keep it monthly to avoid confusion.\n\nActually, let me be careful not to over-produce numbers. The user asked for \"price bands for each.\" I'll give a tight band per project:\n\nacompose (agent-compose):\n- One-time cut: small surface layer, but locked to housecast upstream. $3k–$15k (20–100 hrs @ $150).\n- Long-term support: $750–$2,250/mo (5–15 hrs/mo).\n\numbra:\n- One-time cut: security-critical, deeper integration. $8k–$30k (50–200 hrs @ $150).\n- Long-term support: $1,500–$4,500/mo (10–30 hrs/mo) — security tooling carries higher support because a break means an unguarded agent.\n\nI must flag: these hour estimates are mine; Kai has confirmed no tier, no ladder, no walk-away. The $150 rate is the only confirmed number.\n\n**Would anyone buy — verdict**: Jev (jev-1.13.0) scored buyer-likelihood within 12 months at 0.20 (umbra) and 0.16 (acompose), where the scale runs toward yes=1. Both read as no near-term buyer. So: not today, umbra marginally ahead.\n\nStructural reasons (strongest case against — this is the guardrail's \"strongest case against\" but also the honest \"why not\"):\n1. No buyer-facing artifact. credential-legibility: current surfaces (code, streams, talks) speak to workers, not buyers. A buyer wants \"their problem named + cost of leaving it unfixed.\" That artifact doesn't exist yet. Without it, there's nothing to buy against.\n2. No customers, no flagship, no single product — the portfolio rule forbids claiming otherwise.\n3. mcp-beaver was closed as a product because 13 gateways (Kong, Red Hat, Cerbos, ToolHive, Permit.io) commoditized the guard/generation space. That's a live warning for umbra: the enforcement space is crowded and commoditizing fast.\n4. acompose's custom cut is hostage to housecast upstream — you'd be selling support for a stack you maintain, a support-liability multiplier, not a clean line item.\n5. \"One offer, five cells, fifth cell converts\" — the offer that converts is the origin story, not the mechanism or a price sheet. A price band with no one asking is the wrong instrument.\n\nReversal observation: the thing that would flip this to \"yes, someone buys\" is a named buyer with the problem (an org running an agent with a shell they don't trust) plus a written buyer-facing artifact that names their problem and the cost of inaction — or a warm introduction actually spent on umbra. The wedge reopens (harness conformance, etc.) all key on evidence of two teams paying attention; same shape here.\n\n**What the $150/hr means against break-even**: $150 clears the $137 break-even by ~$13/hr at 70% util. So at the confirmed rate, umbra/acompose work is viable per hour IF the hours materialize; the exposed term is utilisation (worth $1.9M between 70% and 55% in the household plan — but that's private, category-1 adjacent; I should be careful). Actually the utilisation fragility ($1.9M between 70% and 55%) is a household-plan finding. Is it safe to mention? It's Kai's own financial plan, not the metamour's. The wall is specifically \"Category 1... records another adult's finances and her exit age.\" The utilisation figure and break-even are Kai's. I can reference $137/hr and the 70% util as Kai's own anchors. I'll keep the metamour's stuff