          "type": "text",
          "text": "## The call\n\nDo not quote these to a buyer yet. There is no confirmed price band for either project — the only confirmed pricing anchors are **$150/hr at 70% utilisation** (rule rate, 2026-08-24) and the **$137/hr break-even** against the $200k salary lock (recomputed 2026-09-13). A \"custom one-time cut + long-term support\" package is not a confirmed offering, and the portfolio record forbids claiming a customer, a flagship, or a single product. The gate before any of this becomes quotable is a **buyer-facing artifact that names a real buyer's problem and the cost of leaving it unfixed** — that artifact does not exist today.\n\n## Strongest case against, first\n\nPublishing bands is the wrong instrument right now. The current surfaces (code, talks, streams) speak to **workers, not buyers**, and a buyer needs a written statement of *their* problem plus the cost of inaction, not a price sheet. Meanwhile the guard/occlusion space is already commoditizing: mcp-beaver was closed as a product because thirteen gateways (Kong AI Gateway 3.13, Red Hat, Cerbos, ToolHive, Permit.io) now ship declarative, default-deny MCP ACLs. That same crowding is the live threat to umbra's one-time-cut value. And acompose's custom cut is hostage to housecast upstream — selling it means selling support for a stack you maintain, a liability multiplier rather than a clean line item.\n\nThe observation that would reverse this: a **named buyer** running an agent with a shell they don't trust, or a warm introduction actually spent on umbra. That is the only evidence that flips \"no one buys\" to \"someone buys.\"\n\n## Price bands (arithmetic on the $150 rate — the hour counts are my inference, not Kai's)\n\nNo walk-away, premium tier, or negotiation ladder is confirmed; the $150 rate is the single anchor. Bands below are hours × $150.\n\n| | One-time cut | Long-term support (retainer) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **acompose** (renders a housecast roster into named harness specialists) | $3k–$12k (20–80 hrs) | $750–$2,250/mo (5–15 hrs/mo) |\n| **umbra** (occlusion/enforcement: argv, scope tokens, egress proxy, audit log) | $9k–$30k (60–200 hrs) | $1,500–$4,500/mo (10–30 hrs/mo) |\n\numbra prices higher because it is security-critical (a break means an unguarded agent), and its integration is deeper. acompose prices lower and is the weaker standalone line because it sits on housecast. These are my estimates, not a confirmed ladder.\n\n## Would anyone actually buy one — verdict from a separate decision model\n\nI routed the verdict through Jev (jev-1.13.0) rather than deciding it here. On the yes/no \"is there a real paying buyer within 12 months\":\n\n- **umbra: 0.20**\n- **acompose: 0.16**\n\nBoth read as **no near-term buyer**. umbra is marginally ahead; acompose is the weaker of the two. The deciding fact is the one from the audience side: the formats work, but they aim at practitioners, and no artifact carries a buyer's problem with a consequence attached. At $150/hr both clear the $137 break-even per hour, so the economics are viable **if the hours materialize** — the exposed term is utilisation, not rate.\n\n**Gate:** these bands stay a thought exercise, not a quote, until there's a buyer-facing written artifact and an introduction actually spent on umbra. Revisit the moment either of those exists.\n\nOutward note: I checked no outside source — there's no web tool in this session, so I have no market-comparison count to report and I'm not claiming the market pays these numbers; these are Kai's rate applied to my hour estimates."
